# sntpd -- the NTP client. THE PRINTER HAS NO RTC AND NO BATTERY, so every
# boot starts at whatever the kernel calls zero and stays there until
# something asks the network. Nothing on the stock rootfs can ask: its busybox
# is v1.31.1 built WITHOUT the ntpd applet (measured -- it has date, hwclock,
# rdate and crond, and rdate is RFC 868, not NTP).
#
# sntpd rather than ntpd/chrony/OpenNTPd: it is a fork of Larry Doolittle's
# ntpclient, one small daemon with no privsep user, no chroot and no runtime
# config file to ship -- and it keeps running with the network down instead of
# exiting, which is the only behaviour that matters on a machine whose wifi
# comes up minutes after the service does.
SNTPD_VERSION="3.2"
SNTPD_SHA256="15852f86b319c52134e5ffbfa43d38fa0b755a9a8ab18e3b17dfa3e84e000763"
